  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "A **vector** carries two facts at once: a **magnitude** (how much) and a **direction** (which way). In components, v = ⟨a, b⟩ means 'go a right and b up.' Unlike a plain number — a scalar — a vector points somewhere. Velocity, force, and displacement are all vectors.",
      "figure": "vec-components"
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "An arrow has a size and a direction — and components carry both.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "vectorExplore",
        "mode": "add",
        "ux": 0,
        "uy": 0,
        "targetX": 3,
        "targetY": 4,
        "vxStart": 1,
        "vyStart": 0,
        "gridMax": 6,
        "prompt": "u is the zero vector, so u + v is just v. Land the sum on (3, 4) and read the length.",
        "successFeedback": "v = (3, 4), and its length is √(3² + 4²) = 5. The two components are not the vector — they are a recipe for reaching it: 3 across, then 4 up. Length and direction are what the arrow actually IS.",
        "lowFeedback": "The horizontal part is wrong — the sum must land 3 to the right.",
        "highFeedback": "The horizontal part is right but the height is wrong — the sum must land 4 up."
      },
      "cml": {
